<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n74600" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁπόθῐ</span></b> , 
			Ep.
		
		<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁππόθι</span></b>  as always in <i><span class="en">
			Hom.
		</span></i>, poet. Adv., correl. to <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πόθι, ὁππόθι πιότατον πεδίον . .
					,ἔνθα . . τέμενος ἑλέσθαι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					9.577
				</span></i>
			 ; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὁπόθι θάνατος ἀπῇ</span> (or
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐπῇ</span>) <i><span class="en">
				A.
				<u>Supp.</u>
				124
			</span></i> (lyr.), as corrected. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b> used in indirect questions, like the Prose 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὅπου, σάφα εἰπέμεν ὁππόθ᾽ ὄλωλεν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Od.
					3.89
				</span></i>
			 ; 
				v.
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὅθι.</span>
			</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
